WEBVTT

00:00:00.183 --> 00:00:02.555
VRAM often sets the first boundary.

00:00:02.555 --> 00:00:04.600
It must hold model weights, runtime overhead,

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.295
and the key-value cache used by active context.

00:00:07.295 --> 00:00:09.470
When the model fits completely on a supported GPU,

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:12.200
generation usually benefits from high memory bandwidth.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.760
If only part fits, the runtime may split work between GPU and CPU,

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.120
increasing transfers and slowing output.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:22.454
A model that consumes nearly all VRAM can fail as context or batch size grows.

00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:26.755
CPU performance matters for tokenization, prompt processing, unsupported layers,

00:00:26.755 --> 00:00:28.200
and CPU-only inference.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.590
System RAM must hold the operating system, runtime,

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:33.820
model data that does not fit on the GPU, and other applications.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:38.040
Heavy paging to disk can make a configuration technically functional but unusable.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.460
Storage speed affects model loading and cache operations,

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.560
though it does not usually control token generation after the working set is in memory.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:49.070
PCI Express bandwidth matters when model data moves repeatedly between system memory and a

00:00:49.070 --> 00:00:49.890
discrete GPU.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.450
Unified-memory systems share one memory pool between CPU and GPU,

00:00:53.450 --> 00:00:57.280
reducing some transfers but sharing bandwidth and capacity across both.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:00.740
Neither architecture removes limits; each changes where they appear.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:03.590
Observe clocks, utilization, temperature, power, memory,

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:05.350
and storage during the real workload.

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:09.120
Cooling or power limits can reduce sustained clocks after an initially fast run.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:12.310
Background processes can consume RAM or GPU resources.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:15.785
Upgrade only after evidence identifies the constrained stage.

00:01:15.785 --> 00:01:20.180
More RAM will not fix a fully GPU-resident model limited by GPU memory bandwidth,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.212
and a faster GPU will not fix a workload stalled on storage or thermal throttling.
